This is my third ascension in a year of playing, my first genoless
(other two: barb, and valk).

gngstrMNKY the healer was randomly generated after a long fruitless
string of samurai. I was pretty pessimistic at my odds of survival
since I had nopet enabled, but managed to find an elven dagger pretty
early, and got to skilled. Also had good luck with armor. Lots of armor
stores early on gave me speed boots, a T-shirt, and gaunlets of dex
(and power, but I went with dex for 99% of the game) Minetown's altar
was co-aligned and the general store had a few spellbooks, one of which
was create monster. Unfortunately I got crowned very early - at least
it granted me a bunch of resistances, and the oh-so-useful Vorpal
Blade..

Somehow I found FOUR luckstones before even getting to dlvl 7 of the
Mines, but I went to the end anyway for the xp and loot. Once I found a
unicorn horn, I adopted the combat-wombat style I'm so comfortable
with. Sokoban was reflection, and the Oracle level was a wizard bones
file that left me a very very much appreciated cloak of magic
resistance. With both of these and an AC of about -10, I felt
comfortable doing Fort Ludious. Only an orange dragon left scales,
which I enchanted somewhat hesitantly - had found a Magic Marker
somewhere along the way. Better than Studded Leather, that's for sure.
Bought protection with all the loot after killing Croesus in my
customary way - freezing the moat, then melting it and drowning the
wretchedly rich King.

At this point I was already XL 14 and found the Quest portal on the
Rogue level at DLVL 16 - a first for me - with a conveniently located
co-aligned altar at DLVL 15. More sacrificing got me a spellbook of
Magic Missile, stealth, and good ol' Magicbane. Well, that +4 unicorn
horn packeda lot more punch. I had no means of levitation so I froze
the waters on the quest level. I was pretty wary, having been drowned
twice before on the Healer quest, so I stayed my distance and Zapped
missiles at all the pesky ; hanging around. Lulled into a fase sense of
security by the relative ease of the rest of the Quest, I was brought
down a notch when I encountered the Cyclops. I hadn't thought about how
the Staff might affect me.. and the Cyclops damn near killed me,
draining me to XL 11 in the process. I thought fast, and dropped a
Scroll of Scare Monster on the upstairs where I'd been duking it out
with him, hoping it'd work. Well, needless to say, a few more whacks
with the unihorn and the Staff was mine.

I proceeded down to Medusa and dug down, having exhausted my wand of
cold at the quest. Unfortunately, the statue of Perseus lacked the Lev
Boots I'd been hoping for, so I went to the Castle, blew down the
drawbridge with a wand of striking. A conveniently placed winter wolf
froze the moat for me, and after clearing out the inhabitants, I found
the Wand of Wishing. I'd gone wishless up to this point despite finding
two magic lamps, but I was starting to get burdened and wished up a Bag
of Holding at this point, swallowing my dignity. Down at the Valley of
the Dead, I farmed wraiths to recover my XL up to about 24. Realizing
the Staff of Aesculapius' effectiveness was severely hindered against
drain-resistant monsters, I used the rest of my magic marker to enchant
it to +6. Heading to Vlad's for Water Walking Boots, I lost my nerve at
some point when I started running low on Holy Water, so I just wished
up a ring of levitation and headed back up to my altar for some
busy-work.

Onto the interesting parts.. I explored Gehennom by hand lacking even a
pickax, for some masochistic reason. On the one level that I used a
scroll of magic mapping, I found Vlad's Tower and took care of that
wimp. Asmodeus and Juiblex were pushovers, but thanks to some
trapdoors, I fell right through Beelzebub and Orcus. Before I knew it
I'd felt a strange vibration around XL 47 - the highest I've ever found
it. Marked it with some wands, and headed back upstairs.

Here's where I had my encounter with death - and was grateful I'd
picked up that AoLS. Going up, I stumbled into Orcus-town, where
Yeenoghu was promptly summoned. Then a pack of arch-liches showed up
and I was in quite a mess. Still managed to take out Yeenoghu as Orcus
crowded the upstairs - I'd made my way over there painstakingly, just
hanging onto Magicbane so as not to get much cursed. Orcus went
upstairs, I took out a couple arch-liches then followed = right into
Beelzebub's lair, it seemed. I had no gold in my inventory or else I'd
have bribed him because Orcus was really annoying me. Long story short,
I managed to kill him, but then while fighting Beelzebub I got stunned.
Applying my unihorn, I felt very ill.. oh no!! I wasn't thinking too
well at this point because I figured I'd wield the horn and then cast
Remove Curse. Doh! Now I couldn't do much of anything, and I panicked
and just sort of flapped around ineffectually until I died from my
illness. There goes my AoLS..

Still with the problem of the unihorn (I'd cursed-teleported away and
ended up at the Castle), I prayed. Only to piss off Hermes and lose my
protection. OK, so now what? I ended up dropping all my inventory and
zapping myself with a wand of cancellation - goring the AC of my
gauntlets, tshirt, and SDSM (I'd made a pair at the Castle). Now my AC
was crap, but at least I was alive and had an uncursed unihorn.

After recovering from this, I went and killed the priest of Poseidon to
whom I'd donated about 80,000 gp, and used this to buy back most of the
protection I'd lost from Moloch's priest. I consolidated a bunch of
junk, dropped a sack full of gems and other sundries at the upstairs of
DLVL 1, then went back down to Gehennom, killed Beelzebub without much
fanfare, cleared out the Wizard's tower and saved- I wanted to hold off
my final ascension run until I was done travelling and was at home with
my wife, as she always keeps me in check and stops me from playing so
fast and carelessly (thanks, honey!)

So my ascension began with an unglorious battle with Rodney - just
melee'd him to death with my staff, of course. Grabbed the book and
hauled to the Sanctum, where the crux of the story takes place - if
you've made it this far, this was by far the most bizarre NH moment
I've ever had.

After performing the invocation ritual, I descend into the Sanctum.
After taking two steps, Dispater shows up! I'd never even seen him
before in a game, but he wasn't hard at all. What *was* very strange
was that he had the Amulet of Yendor! I was sure it was a fake, but a
scroll of identify proved otherwise. Okay.. well, I felt like this was
a bug or something, and it'd be cheating not to go fight the hordes in
the Sanctum and kill Moloch's high priestes.. But my wife convinced me
not to push my luck and just get out of there.

That annoying mysterious force wasn't nearly as bad as my lawful
ascension - only had to cross Juiblex's swamp twice as opposed to seven
times! All the way up to DLVL 1, and onto the elemental planes -
forgetting my bag o' gems - n - swag, as well as a vitally important,
yet easily overlooked detail - scrolls of gold detection...

By luck I stumbled into the first portal on the Plane of Earth,
spending only a couple hundred turns there. Air wasn't too bad either
because an energy vortex found the portal for me. I came dangerously
close to using my 2nd and last AoLS on the Plane of Fire, though. I was
wearing the Amulet of Yendor to find the portal and staying in the
"very warm" area, when I just started getting wailed on by nasties
who'd been summoned by someone or another - a nalfeshnee I think? I had
no power, wasn't regenerating any either, and wasn't finding the
portal. I had to teleport some baddies away and #invoke the Staff to
stay alive, but then I magically stumbled into the portal! Yay!

On the portal of Water I tried just levitating around - which I'd never
done before out of some irrational fear. Thiis was where I'd most
feared not finding the portal, but it only took about 800 turns or so -
 thanks to the Amulet of Yendor's temperature sensitivity, heh.

Oh, did I mention I'd been damn near starving most of this time? Never
found a ring of slow digestion the entire game, and I'd eaten all my
K-rations by this point. So I was eager to get on with it at the Astral
Plane. Went for Death first. Whoah, his draining attack seemed more
potent than I remembered! Hermes' angel helped me out a little as I
used the summoned insects as shields from Death and the various harder
baddies, forging my way to... Athena's temple. Doh! Next I tried
Pestilence, since I had only a couple food rations for nutrition, and
death had gotten me down to about 260 max HP. That temple was
Poseidon's, of course.. so, onto Famine. Luckily of all the three
riders Famine seemed to be the easiest, dying with only one or two
thwacks of my staff. Into the temple, I decided to fight the Riders a
few more times, just for extra points, but finally, as Death raped me
into HP-loss, and near-death, my wife put her foot down and I realized
it was time to #offer the amulet to Hermes..

